    "# Lab 4: KNN Classification & Evaluation Metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "## Self-Learning Notes\n",
    "- **Feature Scaling Importance**: Learned that KNN is distance-based and requires feature scaling because features with larger scales (e.g., area in thousands vs. texture in tens) would dominate distance calculations, making the algorithm biased toward those features\n",
    "- **Cross-Validation vs Train-Test Split**: Cross-validation provides more reliable performance estimates by using multiple train-test splits, reducing variance in the evaluation compared to a single split\n",
    "- **ROC-AUC Interpretation**: Learned that ROC-AUC measures the model's ability to distinguish between classes across all threshold values, with 0.5 being random guessing and 1.0 being perfect classification"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Aim\n",
    "\n",
    "To implement K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) classification on the Breast Cancer dataset, evaluate model performance using various metrics, compare different train-test splits and K values, and understand the differences between regression and classification evaluation approaches."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Dataset Description\n",
    "\n",
    "The Breast Cancer dataset (brca.csv) contains 569 samples with 30 numerical features computed from digitized images of fine needle aspirates (FNA) of breast masses. The features describe characteristics of cell nuclei present in the image, including:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Mean values**: radius, texture, perimeter, area, smoothness, compactness, concavity, concave points, symmetry, fractal dimension\n",
    "- **Standard error values**: for each of the above features\n",
    "- **Worst values**: mean of the three largest values for each feature\n",
    "\n",
    "**Target variable**: Diagnosis (B = Benign, M = Malignant)\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a binary classification problem where we aim to predict whether a tumor is benign or malignant based on these cellular characteristics."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Problem Statement\n",
    "\n",
    "Breast cancer diagnosis is a critical medical application where accurate classification can significantly impact patient outcomes. This lab focuses on:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Building a KNN classifier to predict tumor malignancy\n",
    "2. Understanding how different train-test splits affect model performance\n",
    "3. Finding the optimal K value for KNN\n",
    "4. Comparing distance metrics (Euclidean vs. Manhattan)\n",
    "5. Evaluating model performance using comprehensive metrics\n",
    "6. Understanding the difference between regression and classification evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "The medical context requires careful consideration of evaluation metrics—false negatives (missing a malignant tumor) are far more dangerous than false positives."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Required Libraries\n",
