    "## Question 2: Observations\n",
    "\n",
    "**Observation:**\n",
    "- All three split ratios achieve high accuracy (>95%), precision (>95%), recall (>95%), and F1-score (>95%)\n",
    "- The 80:20 split achieves the best balance across all metrics\n",
    "- The 90:10 split has the highest training data but lowest test reliability due to small test set\n",
    "- The 70:30 split has the most reliable evaluation but slightly lower performance due to smaller training set\n",
    "\n",
    "**Interpretation:**\n",
    "The small performance differences across splits suggest the KNN model is relatively stable. The 80:20 split represents a good compromise—enough training data to learn patterns (455 samples) and enough test data for reliable evaluation (114 samples). The 90:10 split's high metrics may be optimistic due to the small test set (only 57 samples), while the 70:30 split's slightly lower metrics reflect the challenge of learning from less training data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Practical Insight:**\n",
    "- **Bias**: 70:30 split has higher bias (less training data to learn from)\n",
    "- **Variance**: 90:10 split has higher variance (evaluation depends heavily on which 57 samples are in test set)\n",
    "- **Generalization**: 80:20 split offers the best generalization—balanced bias-variance trade-off\n",
    "- **Model Stability**: Similar performance across splits indicates the model is not overfitting to specific data patterns\n",
    "\n",
    "For medical applications, the 80:20 split is recommended as it provides sufficient training data while maintaining reliable evaluation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Question 3: K Value Experimentation and Distance Metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "### Purpose\n",
    "Find the optimal K value for KNN by experimenting with values around the heuristic K = √n, and compare Euclidean vs. Manhattan distance metrics.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why This Step Is Needed\n",
    "- **K value selection**: Small K values (e.g., K=1) lead to overfitting (model captures noise), while large K values lead to underfitting (model oversmooths decision boundaries)\n",
    "- **Distance metrics**: Different distance measures can capture different notions of similarity, affecting classification performance\n",
    "\n",
    "### Expected Output\n",
    "- Accuracy vs. K graph showing optimal K\n",
    "- Comparison of Euclidean vs. Manhattan distance\n",
    "- Explanation of distance metric formulas and use cases"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Use 80:20 split for K experimentation (best balance from Q2)\n",
