    "### Distance Metrics Explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Euclidean Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "**Formula:**\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d(x, y) = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i - y_i)^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**When to use:**\n",
    "- Default choice for most continuous features\n",
    "- Appropriate when all dimensions are equally important\n",
    "- Works well when features are uncorrelated\n",
    "\n",
    "**Advantages:**\n",
    "- Intuitive geometric interpretation (straight-line distance)\n",
    "- Works well for compact, isotropic clusters\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:**\n",
    "- Sensitive to outliers (squared term amplifies large differences)\n",
    "- Assumes all dimensions are equally important\n",
    "- Can be dominated by features with large scales (mitigated by standardization)\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Manhattan Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "**Formula:**\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d(x, y) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} |x_i - y_i|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**When to use:**\n",
    "- High-dimensional data (less affected by curse of dimensionality)\n",
    "- When features have different importance (can be weighted)\n",
    "- Grid-like data (e.g., city blocks)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Advantages:**\n",
    "- More robust to outliers (absolute value vs. square)\n",
    "- Better for high-dimensional spaces\n",
    "- Can be more interpretable in certain contexts\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations:**\n",
    "- Less intuitive geometric interpretation\n",
    "- May not capture diagonal relationships as well\n",
    "- Can be less accurate for compact clusters"
   ]
