    "### Q6: What does ROC-AUC measure and why is it useful?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Answer:**\n",
    "ROC-AUC measures the model's ability to distinguish between positive and negative classes across all possible classification thresholds. The ROC curve plots true positive rate (recall) vs. false positive rate (1 - specificity) at different thresholds. AUC is the area under this curve, ranging from 0.5 (random guessing) to 1.0 (perfect classification).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Explanation:**\n",
    "- **Threshold Independence**: ROC-AUC evaluates the model across all thresholds, not just the default 0.5. This is important because the optimal threshold depends on the relative costs of false positives vs. false negatives.\n",
    "- **Discriminative Ability**: AUC = 0.99 (our model) means excellent discriminative ability—the model can almost perfectly separate benign from malignant cases.\n",
    "- **Threshold Selection**: The ROC curve helps select the optimal threshold based on clinical priorities (e.g., maximize recall for screening, maximize precision for confirmation).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Real-World Example:**\n",
    "In a credit card fraud detection system, you might use different thresholds for different scenarios:\n",
    "- For real-time transaction blocking: Use a high threshold (low false positive rate) to avoid blocking legitimate transactions\n",
    "- For post-transaction review: Use a lower threshold (high recall) to catch as much fraud as possible for manual review\n",
    "The ROC curve helps visualize the trade-off at different thresholds and select the appropriate one for each scenario."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
