   "source": [
    "### Q7: How does KNN differ from other classification algorithms like Logistic Regression or Decision Trees?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Answer:**\n",
    "KNN is a non-parametric, instance-based learning algorithm, while Logistic Regression is a parametric linear model and Decision Trees are hierarchical rule-based models. KNN makes predictions based on local similarity to training examples, while Logistic Regression learns global weights and Decision Trees learn hierarchical decision rules.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Explanation:**\n",
    "- **KNN**: Non-parametric (no assumptions about data distribution), instance-based (stores all training data), lazy learning (computation at prediction time). Makes predictions based on nearest neighbors. Good for complex, non-linear boundaries but slow at prediction time with large datasets.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Logistic Regression**: Parametric (assumes linear relationship), model-based (learns weights), eager learning (computation at training time). Makes predictions using a linear combination of features passed through a sigmoid function. Fast at prediction time but limited to linear boundaries.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Decision Trees**: Non-parametric, model-based, eager learning. Makes predictions using hierarchical if-then rules. Interpretable and handles non-linear relationships well but prone to overfitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Real-World Example:**\n",
    "- **KNN**: Image recognition where similarity to known examples is intuitive\n",
    "- **Logistic Regression**: Credit scoring where interpretability and speed are important\n",
    "- **Decision Trees**: Medical diagnosis where explainable decision rules are required for regulatory approval\n",
    "\n",
    "For breast cancer diagnosis, KNN works well because the feature space is complex and non-linear, but in practice, ensemble methods like Random Forest or gradient boosting often achieve better performance by combining multiple decision trees."
   ]
