    "## Self Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "### Topic 1: Weighted KNN\n",
    "\n",
    "**Difference between Uniform and Distance-Weighted Voting:**\n",
    "\n",
    "In standard KNN (uniform voting), all K neighbors contribute equally to the classification decision. If K=5 and 3 neighbors are class A and 2 are class B, the prediction is class A regardless of how close each neighbor is.\n",
    "\n",
    "In distance-weighted KNN, closer neighbors have more influence on the decision. Each neighbor's vote is weighted by the inverse of its distance (or another weighting function). A neighbor at distance 1.0 might have weight 1.0, while a neighbor at distance 5.0 might have weight 0.2.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why Weighted KNN Helps:**\n",
    "\n",
    "When neighbors are at different distances, uniform voting gives equal influence to a very close neighbor (high similarity) and a distant neighbor (low similarity). This can lead to poor predictions if the distant neighbor belongs to a different class. Weighted voting ensures that the most similar examples have the greatest influence on the prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Small Practical Example:**\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider classifying a tumor as benign or malignant:\n",
    "- K=3 neighbors: 2 benign at distances 0.5 and 0.6, 1 malignant at distance 0.1\n",
    "- Uniform voting: Predict benign (2 vs 1 votes)\n",
    "- Weighted voting: The malignant neighbor at distance 0.1 has much higher weight (~10x) than the benign neighbors, so the prediction might be malignant\n",
    "\n",
    "The weighted prediction is more intuitive—the very similar malignant case should influence the decision more than the somewhat similar benign cases.\n",
    "\n",
    "**sklearn Parameter:**\n",
    "\n",
    "In scikit-learn's KNeighborsClassifier, use `weights='distance'` to enable distance-weighted voting:\n",
    "```python\n",
    "knn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=10, weights='distance')\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**When to Prefer Weighted KNN:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- When the distribution of neighbors is uneven (some very close, some far)\n",
    "- When closer neighbors should logically have more influence\n",
    "- When dealing with noisy data where distant neighbors might be misleading\n",
    "- In medical applications where similarity to very similar cases should dominate"
   ]
