    "### Topic 2: Curse of Dimensionality in KNN\n",
    "\n",
    "**High-Dimensional Distance Problems:**\n",
    "\n",
    "As the number of features (dimensions) increases, KNN performance can degrade due to the \"curse of dimensionality.\" In high-dimensional spaces:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Distance Concentration**: All points become approximately equidistant from each other. The difference between the nearest and farthest neighbor diminishes, making it hard to distinguish \"close\" from \"far.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Volume Sparsity**: The volume of the space grows exponentially with dimensions. Data becomes sparse, and most points are far apart.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Irrelevant Features**: With many features, some are likely irrelevant or noisy. These features add noise to distance calculations, degrading performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why KNN Performance May Degrade:**\n",
    "\n",
    "KNN relies on the assumption that similar points are close in feature space. In high dimensions, this assumption breaks down because:\n",
    "- Euclidean distance loses its meaning (all distances become similar)\n",
    "- The concept of \"nearest neighbor\" becomes less meaningful\n",
    "- The algorithm may need exponentially more data to maintain performance\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why PCA Can Help:**\n",
    "\n",
    "Principal Component Analysis (PCA) reduces dimensionality by projecting data onto the directions of maximum variance while preserving most information:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Visualization**: PCA to 2D or 3D allows visualization of decision boundaries (as we did in this lab)\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Noise Reduction**: PCA can eliminate low-variance dimensions that are likely noise\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Performance Improvement**: By reducing to the most informative dimensions, PCA can sometimes improve KNN performance by mitigating the curse of dimensionality\n",
    "\n",
    "**Relation to Breast Cancer Dataset:**\n",
    "\n",
    "The Breast Cancer dataset has 30 numerical features, which is moderately high-dimensional. While KNN still performs well (97% accuracy), the curse of dimensionality is a concern:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Many features are correlated (e.g., radius, perimeter, area are all related to tumor size)\n",
    "- Some features may be less diagnostically relevant than others\n",
    "- PCA to 2 components explained ~45% of variance, suggesting significant redundancy\n",
    "\n",
    "In practice, feature selection (choosing the most important features) or dimensionality reduction (PCA) could potentially improve performance and interpretability. However, with 30 features and 569 samples, the curse of dimensionality is not severe enough to prevent good KNN performance."
   ]
